# tinyproxy — the US exit, as redbot needs it
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY AN HTTP PROXY AND NOT A VPN, WHEN A VPN IS WHAT THIS MACHINE IS FOR
#
# redbot's relay speaks HTTP proxy and nothing else: it is built on node:http and hand-writes
# `CONNECT <host:port> HTTP/1.1` to its upstream (src/proxy/relay.ts). `UpstreamProxy` is
# {host, port, username, password} — there is no protocol field, so there is no SOCKS path and
# no tunnel path. The tailnet carries the connection; this daemon is what waits at the end of it.
#
# That is also why Tailscale's exit-node feature is NOT used. An exit node routes the whole
# machine, which would give every account one shared exit and leave `manager.ts` nothing
# per-account to pin. This is a private wire to ONE port.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REPLACE THREE THINGS, THEN NOTHING ELSE
#
#   100.64.0.2   -> this machine's tailnet address        (`tailscale ip -4`)
#   100.64.0.3   -> the operator machine's tailnet address
#   <PASSWORD>   -> a long random password, generated, not chosen
#
# Then run tools/exit/verify-exit.sh before telling anyone it is up.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BINDING — the line that decides whether this is a private exit or an open proxy.
#
# Tinyproxy's default is the wildcard address: "By default, Tinyproxy listens for connections on
# all available interfaces (i.e. it listens on the wildcard address 0.0.0.0)". On a home network
# that default means every device in the house — and anything that reaches the router — can use
# this to browse the internet as this household. Bind to the tailnet address ONLY.
#
# redbot keeps the same rule on its own side and states the reason: its relay "never binds
# anything but 127.0.0.1. A relay reachable from the LAN is an open proxy carrying someone
# else's traffic out through an IP we are paying to keep clean."
Listen 100.64.0.2
Port 8888

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ACCESS — second lock, behind the tailnet.
#
# "If there are no Allow or Deny lines, then all clients are allowed. Otherwise, the default
# action is to deny access." So this single line flips the default to deny, and only the
# operator's node gets through. Belt and braces with `Listen`: if `Listen` is ever edited wrong,
# this still refuses the house.
Allow 100.64.0.3/32

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AUTHENTICATION — not optional, in both directions.
#
# redbot's side: the relay "never starts without a credential", and `proxy vet` refuses when any
# of REDBOT_PROXY_HOST/PORT/USER/PASS is missing. An unauthenticated upstream cannot be used by
# this product even though it would technically work.
#
# This side: a port with no password is one routing mistake away from being an open relay on
# someone's home connection. Two locks are cheap; the failure is not.
#
# VERIFY THE SYNTAX ON THE INSTALLED VERSION. The official documentation gives
# `BasicAuth user password` (space-separated); at least one secondary source gives
# `BasicAuth user:password`, and there is an open upstream issue titled "BasicAuth still not
# working" (tinyproxy#510). verify-exit.sh proves it empirically, which is the only proof that
# counts: an unauthenticated request MUST come back 407. If it does not, stop and switch to
# 3proxy rather than shipping an open proxy.
BasicAuth redbot <PASSWORD>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONNECT — the method that carries essentially everything.
#
# Chrome sends CONNECT for every https:// origin, so without 443 nothing works at all. Listing
# it explicitly also closes the default: "If no ConnectPort line is found, then all ports are
# allowed", which would let this tunnel to any service on any port.
ConnectPort 443

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# QUIET — do not announce what this is.
#
# Via and X-Tinyproxy headers advertise a proxy hop to every origin. Reddit reading a Via header
# is a stronger tell than the address itself: it turns "a US household" into "a US household
# running a proxy".
DisableViaHeader Yes
XTinyproxy No

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LOGGING — as little as does the job.
#
# This machine belongs to somebody who is doing us a favour. Their disk should not fill with a
# record of our browsing, and a connection log is the one artefact here that could embarrass
# both sides. Info level, no ConnectionLog of full URLs.
LogLevel Warning
# LogFile "<the log path your package already set — check the config it shipped>"

# Modest limits — this serves one browser, not a household.
MaxClients 20
Timeout 600

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EGRESS SCOPING — optional, and it protects the HOST more than it protects us.
#
# With a filter file in place this connection cannot be used for anything but Reddit, even by
# accident, even by us. That is a promise worth being able to make to the person whose internet
# this is.
#
# UNVERIFIED against a live redbot collect: scoping too tightly may block an asset domain the
# page needs. Start with these lines COMMENTED OUT, get one successful collect through the exit,
# then enable and re-test. Do not tighten and walk away.
#
# Filter "<absolute path to the file below, beside the config your package shipped>"
# FilterExtended On
# FilterDefaultDeny Yes
#
# allowed-domains.txt, as a starting point:
#   (^|\.)reddit\.com$
#   (^|\.)redd\.it$
#   (^|\.)redditstatic\.com$
#   (^|\.)redditmedia\.com$
